  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nAt 12 PM CDT, skies are mostly sunny. Temperatures are warming \nthrough the 70s and 80s. Northwest winds are increasing to 15 to 30 \nmph with gusts up to 35 to 45 mph, and relative humidity is falling \nthrough the 20 to 40 percent range. Red Flag Warning conditions are \nexpected to begin within the next couple of hours across north \ncentral South Dakota.\n\nA Rex block upper level pattern is taking shape well off the west \ncoast, and is progged to persist for pretty much the entire 7-day \nforecast period. Closer to home, this pattern is helping set the \nstage for a considerably more laminar flow pattern over the northern \nplains. It's not going to be completely zonal aloft throughout the \nperiod, but certainly more fast-moving/transient of features, both \nupper lows and upper ridges.\n\nHigh pressure is in place at the surface for the next several days, \ninterrupted at times, briefly by cold frontal passages. Friday's \ncold frontal passage may happen when enough Gulf moisture has \nreturned to the region to support thunderstorm development along the \nboundary. Current timing places any thunderstorms over this CWA \nclose to the Minnesota/South Dakota border Friday afternoon/evening, \nwith storms moving quickly off into Minnesota. SPC is keeping a \nclose eye on this potential scenario (a 15% severe outlook coverage \narea currently drawn over the far eastern forecast zones in the SPC \nDay 4 Outlook). Otherwise, models appear to be zero'ing in on late \nSaturday night through Monday night for the next chance for \nprecipitation. Models/ensembles heights differences and \nprecipitation clusters analyses indicate a couple of upper lows \ncould work through the region at that time. There is currently a 40 \nto 65 percent chance of at least 0.25 inches of precipitation (an \nincrease in probabilities from yesterday) over this CWA in the \nensembles output for Sunday into Monday.\n\nThe 7-day forecast has become dominated by near to below normal \ntemperature potential, with the flow pattern aloft more inclined to \nbe drawing cooler/drier air, with routine, into this region out of \nCanada. Only Thursday and Friday appear to have the potential for \ntemperatures warmer than normal, when a transient upper level ridge \nworks across the northern plains.",
